# NICE(1)

## NAME

nice - run a command at low priority

## SYNOPSIS

| **nice** [ **-**\&*number* ] command [ arguments ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Nice* executes *command* with low scheduling priority. If the *number* argument is present,
the priority is incremented (higher numbers mean lower priorities) by that amount. The default
*number* is 10. Note: v7's page said the increment was limited to 20. The kernel clamps the
resulting priority to the range 0 through 39 (0 through 2*NZERO-1), not the increment; a nice
value already at 39 does not move.

The super-user may run commands with priority higher than normal by using a negative priority,
e.g. `--10`. A negative *number* from anyone else is silently taken as 0 - the command still
runs, at the priority its caller was entitled to.

Note: v7's page also described *nohup*, a shell script that is not on this system.

## SEE ALSO

nice(2), ps(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Nice* returns the exit status of the subject command.
